data.content:
Here are the recommended replacements using `CollectionAssert` and more specific assertions:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)
    Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0);          // or:
    CollectionAssert.That.IsNotEmpty(users);  // if using MSTest v3+

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive))
    CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(users);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive)); // no built-in predicate overload

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);          // gives better failure messages

    // Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)
    var admin = users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin");
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);
}
```

**Key improvements:**

| Instead of | Use | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.AreNotEqual(0, x.Count)` or just keep `Assert.IsTrue` | Better failure message |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, x.Count)` | Shows expected vs actual on failure |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` | Clearer intent & message |
| `Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(x))` | `CollectionAssert.Contains(list, x)` | Purpose-built |

**Other useful `CollectionAssert` methods:**

- `CollectionAssert.AreEqual(expected, actual)` — same elements, same order
- `CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expected, actual)` — same elements, any order
- `CollectionAssert.Contains(collection, element)`
- `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique(collection)`
- `CollectionAssert.IsSubsetOf(subset, superset)`

For predicate-based checks like `.All(u => u.IsActive)`, MSTest doesn't have a built-in equivalent—`Assert.IsTrue` with LINQ is fine there, or consider adding a helper/using FluentAssertions (`users.Should().OnlyContain(u => u.IsActive)`).
